# Product Specification

## Overview

Artichales is an offline-first academic writing and publishing system centered on a Markdown-first workflow and delivered primarily as a Chrome extension editor. It uses a shadcn-compatible component architecture and a plugin-based pipeline to produce two render targets from a single source: print (PDF) and web (HTML/React). The initial product is intentionally local-first and focuses on a single current document.

Authoring inputs are a single Markdown file and a single BibTeX file. The same Markdown source renders to both print output (templating → PDF) and web output (templating → HTML as a React surface). Templates are selected by a single string name and resolved from `templates/<name>_print.json` and `templates/<name>_web.json`.

**Inputs**
- Markdown document body (single file)
- gray-matter frontmatter
- BibTeX entries (single `.bib` file)
- local assets referenced by `type` and `path`
- selected template name (string; resolves to print and web template JSON files)
- enabled built-in plugins (from `plugins/`)
- selected editor and render themes (initially optional)

**Outputs**
- full print preview
- full web preview
- partial rendered fragments
- validated citation graph and bibliography
- PDF download
- HTML output (web target)
- publishable print artifact
- publishable web page
- extension-compatible editor surface
- shadcn-compatible component layer for host applications

## Purpose and Scope

Artichales addresses the gap between traditional academic authoring tools and modern web-native writing systems. Existing academic workflows are often rigid, heavyweight, and difficult to adapt to modern publishing targets. Modern web editors are easier to use but usually weak in citation workflows, print (paper/PDF) rendering, controlled reference models, and reusable publishing pipelines.

Artichales solves this by using Markdown as the canonical authoring source and building a shared parsing, rendering, and extension system around it. The same source should support editing, previewing, exporting, embedding, and publishing. The editor should be usable inside a standard web app, inside host applications that use the shadcn component ecosystem, and inside a Chrome extension package without forking the authoring core.

**Solutions**
- a shared authoring core for editor, preview, export, and publishing
- print (paper/PDF) and web rendering from the same source document
- a theme system that works for editor UI and render targets
- partial rendering for fast preview updates and reusable output fragments
- a controlled BibTeX workflow with validation and editor support
- a plugin system for academic features and host-specific extensions
- a component model that can be integrated into shadcn-based applications
- a build and packaging model that supports Chrome extension delivery

**Scopes**
- offline-first document editing (Chrome extension)
- live preview for print and web targets
- theme-aware rendering
- plugin registration and plugin configuration
- controlled BibTeX ingestion and bibliography generation
- partial rendering for editor and publishing
- embeddable React-based editor surfaces
- Chrome extension packaging
- shadcn-compatible component integration
- export and publish pipelines for print and web

**Out of scope for the initial version**
- real-time collaborative editing
- unrestricted support for all BibTeX entry types
- full WYSIWYG page editing
- cloud-first dependency as a required mode
- full journal workflow management
- runtime plugin downloading or remote plugin loading (built-in plugins only)
- arbitrary plugin execution from untrusted remote sources
- multi-document libraries (only the current single document)

## Goals

Output should be a single-source academic document system that produces stable print and web artifacts from the same Markdown input.

Output should preserve academic structure across all render targets, including citations, bibliography, equations, figures, tables, captions, section numbering, and cross-references.

Output should support both full-document rendering and partial rendering so preview, export, and publishing can reuse the same core without unnecessary work.

Output should expose reusable editor and preview surfaces that can be embedded into standard React applications, Chrome extension UIs, and shadcn-based product surfaces.

Output should keep theme definitions explicit and portable so editor UI and render outputs can be styled consistently without changing content syntax.

UX should be predictable for Markdown users and compact enough for serious writing work.

UX should allow authors to switch instantly between print preview and web preview while keeping a single source of truth.

UX should keep reference editing strict enough to prevent broken academic output but simple enough to remain understandable.

UX should allow theme switching, template switching, and plugin toggling without corrupting document content.

UX should support offline authoring, local recovery, and fast incremental updates as first-class behavior.

## Core Principles

Artichales is source-first. The document source is the permanent input. All previews, exports, and published outputs must be derived from the same source model.

Artichales is Markdown-first. Basic writing remains standard Markdown. Academic behavior is added through parsing, validation, indexing, and plugins rather than by replacing Markdown with a custom authoring language.

Artichales is metadata-aware through gray-matter. Frontmatter stores document metadata and render configuration such as the selected template name (resolved to print and web template JSON files), theme identifiers, and reference source configuration. Temporary preview state is not part of document content.

Artichales treats editor, preview, theme, and plugins as separate but coordinated layers. The editor manages author input, preview renders target-specific output, themes define visual behavior, and plugins extend parsing and rendering capabilities. None of these layers should require the source syntax to change in order to work.

Artichales supports two render targets as first-class outputs. Print and web are not modes of the document. They are render targets produced from the same parsed document model.

Artichales supports partial rendering. The core must be able to render the full document, the abstract, the body, the references, specific sections, and target-specific blocks without always re-rendering the full output. Partial rendering is required for responsive preview, efficient export, and modular publishing integration.

Artichales keeps themes structured. Themes are not only color skins. A theme may define editor UI tokens, print rendering rules, web rendering rules, typography, spacing, code appearance, and block-level presentation. Themes must be packageable and configurable. Themes must be compatible with Tailwind v4 tokens and usable inside shadcn-based hosts.

Artichales keeps plugins controlled. Plugins may extend parsing and rendering behavior, but execution order must be deterministic and configuration must be explicit. In the initial version, only built-in plugins defined under `plugins/` can be enabled and executed. Plugin contract details will be finalized later in `PLUGIN_SPEC.md`.

Artichales keeps references strict. BibTeX is the external reference format, but the internal reference model is normalized and schema-limited. The initial supported entry types are `article`, `book`, `inproceedings`, and `online`.

**Detailed features**
- Markdown editor with local persistence
- print preview and web preview
- full render and partial render
- theme selection and theme overrides
- template selection by name (resolves to `templates/<name>_print.json` and `templates/<name>_web.json`)
- plugin registry and plugin lifecycle
- BibTeX import, parse, normalize, validate, and bibliography render
- citation linking and cross-reference indexing
- figures, tables, captions, equations, and academic blocks
- embeddable React surfaces
- Chrome extension packaging
- shadcn-compatible component composition

**Input definitions**
- raw Markdown content
- frontmatter metadata
- BibTeX text or normalized reference entries
- theme identifiers and optional theme overrides
- template name (string; resolves to print and web template JSON files)
- plugin definitions and plugin configuration
- local assets
- editor interactions
- preview target selection
- export and publish actions

**Output definitions**
- normalized document model
- document indexes
- full print render
- full web render
- partial rendered fragments
- validated references
- citation map
- bibliography output
- PDF artifact
- HTML artifact
- host-mountable editor and preview surfaces

## Architecture

**Complete scheme**

Artichales is organized around a shared document core and several delivery surfaces. The shared core owns frontmatter parsing, Markdown parsing, plugin execution, BibTeX processing, indexing, theming resolution, and target rendering. The editor application, embedded host components, publishing integration, and Chrome extension all consume this shared core.

The editor layer is responsible for document editing, local persistence, preview switching, theme switching, plugin controls, and export actions. The preview layer is responsible for target rendering and fragment rendering. The theme layer resolves UI tokens and render styling for both editor and output surfaces. The plugin layer extends core behavior through controlled hooks. The integration layer packages these surfaces for web apps, shadcn-based applications, and Chrome extension runtimes.

**Folder structure**
```txt
templates/
  <name>_print.json
  <name>_web.json
plugins/
  *.tsx
src/
  ...extension UI + shared core...
```

Pipeline structure

Document parse pipeline
read Markdown source and frontmatter
parse frontmatter with gray-matter
parse Markdown into an intermediate syntax tree
run registered parser plugins in a deterministic order
parse and normalize BibTeX reference input
validate references against supported schemas
build the normalized document model
build document indexes for headings, citations, figures, tables, and cross-references
Full render pipeline
load normalized document model
resolve render target as print or web
resolve selected template name into template files:
- `templates/<name>_print.json`
- `templates/<name>_web.json`
resolve selected theme and theme token overrides
run render plugins
generate target HTML structure
attach target styles, assets, and metadata
return full render result
Partial render pipeline
load normalized document model
determine affected fragment from editor changes or render request
resolve fragment dependencies such as citations, numbering, and cross-references
re-render only the requested fragment or affected render region
update preview surface or host component
preserve full-document indexes unless invalidated by structural changes
Editor pipeline
ingest user input
persist draft locally
debounce parse requests
update diagnostics
perform partial or full preview render
refresh editor and preview UI state
Extension pipeline
mount shared editor or preview surface inside popup, options page, or content script host
use shared core for parsing and rendering
autosave current Markdown and BibTeX into extension local storage for offline persistence
expose export, preview, and insertion actions through extension UI
Shadcn integration pipeline
expose editor and preview primitives as reusable React components
wrap primitives in host-compatible composable components
align theme tokens with Tailwind and shadcn token expectations
allow host apps to compose Artichales surfaces into their own layouts without forking logic
Models

Model definitions

DocumentFrontmatter

Defines source-level metadata and render configuration.

Fields

title: string
authors: Author[]
keywords?: string[]
template: string
editorTheme?: string
printTheme?: string
webTheme?: string
references?: ReferenceSource
plugins?: PluginConfigMap
Author

Defines an author record.

Fields

name: string
affiliation?: string
email?: string
orcid?: string
DocumentSource

Represents raw authoring input.

Fields

frontmatter: DocumentFrontmatter
markdown: string
assets: Asset[]
bibtex: string

Asset

Represents a local asset reference.

Fields

type: string
path: string

Asset upload, management, and richer metadata are deferred and will be specified later in ASSETS_SPEC.md.
DocumentModel

Represents the normalized parsed document.

Fields

frontmatter: DocumentFrontmatter
content: string
blocks: DocumentBlock[]
references: ReferenceEntry[]
indexes: DocumentIndexes
diagnostics: Diagnostic[]
DocumentIndexes

Stores computed indexes needed by rendering.

Fields

headings: HeadingIndex[]
figures: FigureIndex[]
tables: TableIndex[]
citations: CitationIndex[]
crossReferences: CrossReferenceIndex[]
fragments: FragmentIndex[]
FragmentIndex

Defines a renderable fragment and its dependencies.

Fields

id: string
type: "document" | "abstract" | "body" | "references" | "section" | "figure" | "table"
sourceRange: SourceRange
dependsOn: string[]
invalidates: string[]
ThemeDefinition

Defines a packageable theme.

Fields

id: string
target: "editor" | "print" | "web"
tokens: Record<string, string>
typography?: TypographyConfig
spacing?: SpacingConfig
blockStyles?: Record<string, unknown>
codeStyles?: Record<string, unknown>
overrides?: Record<string, unknown>

Theme behavior

editor themes style application UI, panels, toolbars, and document shell
print themes style print-oriented output, typography, spacing, and academic blocks
web themes style browser-oriented output, interactive layout, and responsive presentation
themes may inherit from base themes and override only selected tokens or block definitions
TemplateDefinition

Defines a render target template.

Fields

id: string
target: "print" | "web"
layout: string
render: (model, theme) => RenderResult
slots?: Record<string, string>
assets?: unknown[]

Template resolution (initial version)

the selected template name is a string and must resolve to two files under templates/
templates/<name>_print.json
templates/<name>_web.json

all PDF-relevant layout requirements must be defined by the print template
PluginDefinition

Defines a typed extension unit.

Fields

id: string
name: string
kind: "parser" | "render" | "theme" | "editor" | "integration"
order: number
enabledByDefault: boolean
configSchema?: unknown
hooks: PluginHooks

Plugin behavior

parser plugins can transform syntax trees or document models
render plugins can modify target output or inject structured blocks
theme plugins can extend token sets or block styles
editor plugins can add commands, tool panels, or diagnostics
integration plugins can connect to host surfaces such as extension actions or embeddable APIs

Plugin loading (initial version)

plugins are built-in only and must be defined under `plugins/` as importable `*.tsx` modules
the extension UI allows selecting from the installed plugin list
there is no runtime plugin download and no sandbox model in the initial version
plugin contract details will be specified later in PLUGIN_SPEC.md

Authoring syntax (initial)

Citations are expressed inline and resolved during render using the citation plugin
single citation: [cite:coghlan]
multiple citations: [cite:coghlan, cite:brown]

Tables are expressed using directive blocks and routed to table plugins
:::table routes to the default table plugin
:::table[tabularx] routes to the tabularx table plugin
table data payload format will be specified later in PLUGIN_SPEC.md

Examples

```md
:::table
...table data...
:::

:::table[tabularx]
...table data...
:::
```
ReferenceEntry

Represents a normalized internal BibTeX record.

Fields

id: string
type: "article" | "book" | "inproceedings" | "online"
title: string
authors?: string[]
year: string
fields: Record<string, string>

BibTeX rules

article requires author, title, journal, year
book requires author, title, publisher, year
inproceedings requires author, title, booktitle, year
online requires title, url, year, and either author or organization-equivalent source data
unknown entry types are rejected at validation time in the initial version
unsupported fields are preserved only if the schema explicitly allows them
bibliography output must always use normalized internal models rather than raw BibTeX strings
RenderRequest

Defines a render operation.

Fields

target: "print" | "web"
mode: "full" | "partial"
fragmentId?: string
templateName: string
themeId?: string
pluginOverrides?: Record<string, unknown>
RenderResult

Represents a render response.

Fields

html: string
css?: string
assets?: unknown[]
fragments?: Record<string, string>
diagnostics?: Diagnostic[]
User Flow

UX flows

Author editing flow
open or create a document
edit frontmatter, Markdown content, and references
switch between print preview and web preview
change theme or template selections
continue editing with immediate preview updates
export or publish when ready
Theme flow
select editor, print, or web theme
preview changes against current content
save theme selection into document metadata or host configuration
keep content unchanged while visual output updates
Reference flow
import BibTeX or create a controlled reference entry
validate the entry against the supported schema
insert citation identifiers into content
inspect bibliography output in preview
resolve validation errors before export or publish
Plugin flow
open plugin panel
enable or disable allowed plugins
configure plugin options
trigger re-parse or re-render when plugin behavior affects output
inspect diagnostics if plugin configuration is invalid
Chrome extension flow
open extension UI in popup or options page
create or edit document content using the shared editor surface
preview print or web output inside extension UI
export content or inject output into host pages if enabled
preserve local state across extension sessions
Shadcn host integration flow
install Artichales UI package and core package
compose editor or preview surfaces inside a host layout
connect host theme tokens and application state
render document content through shared core without rewriting components
expose export and publish actions from the host application

System flows

Parse flow
read source
parse frontmatter
parse Markdown
run parser plugins
parse BibTeX
validate models
build indexes
store diagnostics
Preview flow
determine target and fragment scope
resolve template and theme
render full or partial output
update UI surfaces
Theme flow
resolve base theme
apply target-specific theme tokens
merge host overrides
pass resolved theme to render pipeline
refresh affected UI and render surfaces
Plugin flow
load registry
resolve enabled plugins
validate plugin configuration
run plugin hooks in declared order
attach results to parse or render pipeline
